            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Gordon Moore</strong><br>\nGordon Moore is a prominent figure in the field of technology and co-founder of Intel Corporation, one of the world's leading semiconductor chip makers. Born on January 3, 1929, in San Francisco, Moore studied chemistry at the University of California, Berkeley, and received his Ph.D. in chemistry and physics from the California Institute of Technology. He made significant contributions to the development of semiconductors during his career.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Early Career and Founding of Intel</strong><br>\nBefore Intel, Moore worked at Shockley Semiconductor Laboratory and later co-founded Fairchild Semiconductor in 1957, where he developed the planar process. This process was vital for mass-producing silicon semiconductor chips. In 1968, Moore co-founded Intel Corporation with Robert Noyce, with the intention of creating semiconductor memory products.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Moore's Law</strong><br>\nIn 1965, Moore observed that the number of transistors on a microchip doubled every year and predicted that this trend would continue for the foreseeable future. This observation, known as Moore's Law, has been a guiding principle for the semiconductor industry, influencing the pace of computing power growth and technological development.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Impact of Moore's Law</strong><br>\nMoore's Law has had a profound impact on various technology sectors, enabling the steady miniaturization and performance enhancement of electronic devices. It set expectations for the industry, driving innovation and research to maintain the pace of semiconductor complexity and capabilities.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Intel's Growth and Innovation</strong><br>\nUnder Moore's leadership, Intel introduced groundbreaking products, including the world's first microprocessor, the Intel 4004, in 1971. This invention was a catalyst for the personal computer revolution and established Intel as a key player in the computer industry.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Philanthropy and Environmentalism</strong><br>\nApart from his technological contributions, Moore is also known for his philanthropic efforts. He and his wife established the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, which supports environmental conservation, science, and healthcare projects. Moore's commitment to environmental sustainability has been reflected in his business practices and philanthropic priorities.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
